questions.question:
Consider the following HTML and CSS declarations:
```html
<style>
  .alert {
    color: red;
  }
  .warning {
    color: blue;
  }
</style>
<p class="warning alert">Registration deadline approaching!</p>
```
What color will the paragraph text be rendered on screen, and why?

case_studies.scenario:
A frontend developer on the Nile University student portal project is tasked with updating the submit button color to red for the 'Drop Course' warning action. The developer adds a new CSS class:
```css
.btn-danger {
  background-color: red;
}
```
And applies it to the HTML button: `<button id="confirm-drop" class="btn-danger">Drop Course</button>`.
However, the button stubbornly remains GREEN. Upon inspecting the styles in Chrome DevTools, the developer finds an existing rule in the core stylesheet:
```css
#confirm-drop {
  background-color: green;
}
```
A frustrated junior developer suggests appending `!important` to `.btn-danger`.

A student builds a responsive portal for Nile University with nested cards and sidebars. In their CSS, the student uses `em` units for all typography:
```css
body { font-size: 16px; }
.card { font-size: 1.25em; }
.comment { font-size: 1.25em; }
.reply { font-size: 1.25em; }
```
When comments have nested replies (a reply inside a comment inside a card), the text in the innermost reply balloons to a gigantic, distorted size on screen.

sub_questions.answer:
1. Refactoring to `rem` Units:
By replacing `em` with `rem`, font sizes are computed relative to the root `<html>` font size (default 16px) rather than the parent. This permanently eliminates the compounding bug, guaranteeing that `.reply` text remains a predictable, readable size regardless of nesting depth:
```css
:root {
  font-size: 16px;
}

body {
  font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
  font-size: 1rem; /* Exactly 16px */
  line-height: 1.5;
}

.card {
  font-size: 1rem;
}

.comment {
  font-size: 0.95rem; /* ~15.2px */
}

.reply {
  font-size: 0.9rem; /* ~14.4px - stays clean regardless of nesting! */
}
```

2. Robust System Font Stack:
- `font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;`
- `-apple-system` and `BlinkMacSystemFont` target modern Apple macOS/iOS system fonts (San Francisco).
- `'Segoe UI'` targets modern Windows 10/11 system font.
- `Roboto` targets Android and ChromeOS.
- `Helvetica` and `Arial` serve as universal web-safe cross-platform fallbacks.
- The generic `sans-serif` keyword ensures that even if all named fonts fail to load, the browser will render a clean, proportional sans-serif typeface.
